There are generally multiple vectors you can take when it comes to hacking a computer network, but there is a very straightforward and simple approach that is highly likely to be the most successful for you: you should take a multi-step approach of discovery, identification, and elimination of any potential security risks, and then execute the same principles on your own system in order to ensure your own security, and that of the systems you are responsible for: 

\begin{list} {Steps \textit{to} Hack \textit{a} \textit{Network} [\textit{in no} \textit{particular} \textit{order}]}
\item[\textit{Step 1:}\/] \label{step:discovery} Do \textit{not} under any circumstances log into your network or systems as an administrator or with your own credentials; instead, use the same means you would use to discover your own credentials to discover the credentials of the administrator or other privileged user;
\item[\textit{Step 2:}\/] \label{step:identification} Identify all of the components of the network, including any routers, firewalls, servers, and other devices;
